[125.50687, "i", "ker.parse_args([\"--batch-size\", \"0\"])\nPY\ncat >> README.md <<'MD'\n\n## Resume / durability protocol\n\nThe checkpoint's `last_id` is the **last committed** source ID; the next query\nstarts strictly after it (IDs need not be contiguous). Each batch, or partial\nbatch on SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP, is committed in this order:\n\n1. Flush and fsync all affected shards, including shards closed by rotation.\n2. Fsync the index directory and record committed shard names and byte sizes.\n3. Write and fsync a temporary checkpoint, atomically replace the old checkpoint,\n   then fsync its directory.\n\nOn restart, validate the committed files, truncate any bytes past those sizes,\nand remove shards absent from the checkpoint before continuing. Thus a SIGKILL\nbetween writing rows and publishing progress only replays uncommitted work;\npartial JSON/UTF-8 tails are discarded without parsing them. Missing/shortened\ncommitted files or malformed checkpoints fail loudly instead of skipping work.\nA completed rerun performs recovery but does not"]
[125.506952, "o", "import signal\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> import subprocess\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> import sys\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[125.507035, "i", " reindex documents.\n\nOld checkpoints (without shard byte sizes) cannot be trusted: the old worker could\npublish unwritten rows. These are rebuilt once. As before, a missing checkpoint\nmeans a rebuild. The source corpus must remain fixed during a reindex; use\n`./jobs clean` to start a new generation after source changes. Run writers through\n`./jobs reindex` so the supervisor serializes access. Index row transformation is\nunchanged.\nMD\n./jobs test > /tmp/search-412-tests.log 2>&1; cat /tmp/search-412-tests.log\n"]
